  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and I've enabled the rule. I haven't opened the dashboard or waited for the rule to run, so I haven't confirmed that the panels show data or that the rule fires.\n\n**Fields:** The data view `kibana_sample_data_logs` has `timestamp` as its time field. The main fields are:\n- `response` (a string with a `.keyword` version)\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `clientip`\n- `machine.os`\n- `request` and `url`\n- `host` and `agent`\n\n**Dashboard:** The dashboard is `EFF-42ce6887 Web logs overview` ([link](http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-42ce6887-web-logs-overview-918062f08308)). It has 5 panels:\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Requests over time (area chart)\n- Responses by status code (pie)\n- Average bytes by extension (bar)\n- Top source countries (table)\n\n**Rule:** The rule is `EFF-42ce6887 5xx spike` (id `f416e86d-a3a1-49fe-8745-5942683fde8c`), an Elasticsearch query rule.\n- It runs every minute.\n- It looks at the last 5 minutes of `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It counts documents where `response.keyword` matches the regex `5[0-9][0-9]`.\n- It fires when that count is above 10.\n- It was created disabled by default, so I enabled it.\n- It has no action attached, so when it fires it only records the alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone.\n- The sample data's timestamps are shifted to look recent only when it is loaded, so the rule may find few or no matching documents.",
